E=[
(3,1884,"Cosmos","A brilliant point on Venus","M. Staevert of the Brussels Observatory sees an extremely brilliant point on the disc of Venus. Nine days later Niesten sees a similar point of light there. Fort cites Ciel et Terre.","NL","New Lands, citing Ciel et Terre, 5-127","Upon Feb. 3, 1884, M. Staevert, of the Brussels Observatory"),
(4,1871,"Falls","A meteorite and dead fishes in Peru","Near a river in Peru a meteorite falls, and 'several dead fishes' of different species are found on the spot. Nature offers that the fish had been lifted out of the river. Fort enjoys the correlation.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 3-512","near the bank of a river, in Peru, Feb. 4, 1871, a meteorite fell"),
(5,1849,"Cosmos","Two dark bodies seen by Brown of Deal","Brown of Deal reports two dark bodies. Fort lists the sighting in a run of unidentified objects seen crossing the Sun between 1839 and 1849.","NL","New Lands, citing Rec. Sci., 1-138","two dark bodies that were seen, Feb. 5, 1849, by Brown, of Deal"),
(7,1895,"Sky","Sounds and lights over Florida","Investigators of the Florida Weather Service confirm mysterious sounds and luminous effects in the sky around noon on Feb. 7, again at 5 a.m. on the 8th, and again that night. The Review's editor suggested three meteors had exploded in succession.","NL","New Lands, citing Monthly Weather Review","about noon, Feb. 7, 1895"),
(8,1855,"Cryptids","The Devil's Footprints","In the morning, residents of many towns and the country between them wake to find a long trail of strange footprints in new snow. Fort quotes a report of 'a most strange and mysterious description' and notes the marks followed no sensible route.","BD","Book of the Damned, quoting the 1855 account","discovered in the morning of Feb. 8, 1855, in the snow"),
(9,1913,"Sky","The Great Meteor Procession","A line of bright bodies with long tails moves across the sky over Canada, the United States, Bermuda and ships at sea. Prof. Chant of Toronto collected the observations. Fort lingers on the way the bodies kept formation.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Jour. Roy. Astro. Soc. of Canada, Nov.-Dec. 1913","there appeared, upon the night of Feb. 9, 1913, a spectacle"),
(10,1896,"Sky","Explosion over Madrid","A tremendous explosion in the sky over Madrid smashes windows across the city and throws down a wall of the American Legation. Fort uses it to show that something exploding overhead can shake the ground.","NL","New Lands","Upon Feb. 10, 1896, a tremendous explosion occurred in the sky of Madrid"),
(11,1859,"Falls","Fishes fall at Mountain Ash","Fish fall from the sky at Mountain Ash in the Aberdare Valley, Glamorgan. Fort calls it the best-known fall of fishes, and notes the Zoologist's editor says he is 'continually receiving similar accounts of frogs and fishes'.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing The Zoologist, 2-677","Mountain Ash, in the Valley of Abedare, Glamorganshire, Feb. 11, 1859"),
(12,1870,"Sky","Light, quake and sand over Italy","On Feb. 12 and 13, a luminous body in the sky, an earthquake and a fall of sand are reported in Italy. Fort lists it with other cases pairing a luminous object overhead with a quake.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing La Science Pour Tous, 15-159","luminous body in the sky, earthquake, and fall of sand, Italy, Feb. 12 and 13, 1870"),
(13,1836,"Cosmos","Straight lines of light on the Moon","Gruithuisen sees two straight lines of light in the western crater of Messier on the Moon, with a dark band between them covered in luminous points. Fort counts it among lunar 'signals'.","NL","New Lands, citing Sci. Amer. Sup., 7-2629","in the western crater of Messier"),
(14,1915,"Sky","Three aeroplanes over the St. Lawrence","At 10 p.m. three aeroplanes are reported crossing the St. Lawrence near Morristown, N.Y. Canadian police thought they were only fire-balloons. This was before any aircraft were expected to cross the border.","NL","New Lands, citing New York Tribune, Feb. 15, 1915","three aëroplanes had been seen to cross the St. Lawrence river"),
(15,1837,"Falls","Black powder over Comrie","A black powder falls on the Comrie region of Perthshire, Scotland, a district of long-running earthquake-like sounds. Fort cites the Edinburgh New Philosophical Journal.","NL","New Lands, citing Edin. New Phil. Jour., 31-293","Upon Feb. 15, 1837, a black powder fell upon the Comrie region"),
(16,1883,"Falls","The hand-print meteorite of Brescia","An object falls near Brescia, Italy. A false report circulated that one of its fragments bore the impress of a hand. Fort, as he often does, finds the rumour more interesting than the stone.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Scientific American, 48-261","that fell Feb. 16, 1883, near Brescia, Italy"),
(17,1841,"Falls","Resinous fall at Genoa","On Feb. 17 to 19 a substance said to be resinous falls at Genoa. Arago called it bituminous matter and sand. Fort lists it among falls of carbonaceous matter from the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Arago, OEuvres, 12-469","Feb. 17, 18, 19, 1841, at Genoa, Italy"),
(18,1824,"Falls","A five-pound stone at Irkutsk","A stone weighing five pounds falls from the sky at Irkutsk, Siberia, on Feb. 18 or, by other accounts, May 14. Fort connects it to the sounds and shocks reported there that year.","NL","New Lands, citing Rept. B. A., 1860-70","Upon Feb. 18, or, according to other accounts, upon May 14, a stone that weighed five pounds"),
(19,1830,"Falls","A fall of fishes at Feridpoor","According to testimony taken before a magistrate, many fishes of various sizes fall near Feridpoor, India, some whole and fresh and others mutilated and putrefying.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Amer. Jour. Sci., 1-32-199","a fall occurred, Feb. 19, 1830, near Feridpoor, India, of many fishes"),
(20,1877,"Cosmos","A fine line in Eudoxus","M. Trouvelot of the Meudon Observatory sees a fine line in the lunar crater Eudoxus. Fort notes it was during the opposition of Mars.","NL","New Lands","Upon the night of Feb. 20, 1877, M. Trouvelot"),
(24,1868,"Falls","Five hundred tons of dark dust at London, Ontario","In a violent storm a dark substance falls with snow over a belt 50 miles by 10 miles, estimated at 500 tons. Dr. A. T. Machattie examined it under a microscope. Fort cites the Chemical News.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Chemical News, 35-183","at London, Ontario, Feb. 24, 1868, in a violent storm, fell, with snow"),
(25,1858,"Haunts","Phantom soldiers at Mohan","Lord Roberts sees 'phantom soldiers' at Mohan, India, in what he later recounts in Forty-One Years in India. Fort notes the figures wore Hindoo costumes, and asks whether he saw indistinctly or the world is stranger than assumed.","NL","New Lands, citing Lord Roberts, Forty-One Years in India, p. 219","(Forty-One Years in India, p. 219) at Mohan, Feb. 25, 1858"),
(27,1877,"Falls","The golden-yellow fall of Peckloh","A golden-yellow fall at Peckloh, Germany, is found to be coloured by four kinds of organisms, not pollen: tiny arrows, coffee beans, horns and disks. Fort says they might have been symbols.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, May 1877","a golden-yellow fall, of Feb. 27, 1877, at Peckloh, Germany"),
(29,1892,"Sky","The Lyons light, again","The recurring light in the sky above Lyons, N.Y., appears again on a leap day. Dr. M. A. Veeder calls it auroral, and Fort sees possible signalling in its 27-night schedule.","NL","New Lands, citing Scientific American, May 7, 1892","Jan. 5, Feb. 2, Feb. 29, March 27, April 23, 1892"),
]
